export const PLAYBOOK_MARKDOWN = {
  clicked_link: "# Playbook: clicked_link — \"I clicked a link\"\n\nUse when the person opened a link from a suspicious email, text, social media message, or pop-up. Find out quickly: did they only open the page, or did they also type something (password, card, code) or download/install something? If they typed a password, also follow entered_password. If they installed an app or let someone connect, also follow device_compromised.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Close the page or tab. Do not type anything more into it, and do not call any number or reply to any chat it shows.\n2. If a file downloaded, do not open it. Delete it from Downloads (on a phone, do not install anything it asks for).\n3. If they entered a password, card number, or code on that page, say so now: those steps come first (change the password on the real site, or call the number on the back of the card).\n4. If a scary pop-up says the device is locked or infected, it is almost always fake. Close the browser; on a computer, restart it if the page will not close.\n\n## Then\n- Only opened the page: in most cases nothing else is needed. Clear the browser's recent history and site data for that day if they want peace of mind.\n- Check that the browser and operating system are up to date (Settings → Software Update on iPhone/iPad and Mac; Settings → System → Software update on Android; Windows Update on a PC). Updates close the holes that malicious pages rely on.\n- On a computer, run the built-in scan: Microsoft Defender on Windows (Windows Security → Virus & threat protection → Quick scan). Macs scan automatically; keeping macOS updated is the main step.\n- Review browser extensions and remove anything they do not recognize.\n- If they are not sure what they typed, check their important accounts for new sign-ins: Google Security Checkup (https://myaccount.google.com/security-checkup), Apple Account devices (https://account.apple.com), Microsoft recent activity (https://account.live.com/Activity).\n\n## Report\n- Suspicious text message: forward it to 7726 (spells SPAM on the keypad), then delete it.\n- Email: use \"Report phishing\" in Gmail or Outlook, or forward Apple-themed scams to reportphishing@apple.com.\n- The web page itself: report it to Google Safe Browsing at https://safebrowsing.google.com/safebrowsing/report_phish/.\n- Anything that cost money or data: FTC at https://reportfraud.ftc.gov (US). Outside the US, their country's consumer protection agency (UK: Action Fraud; Canada: Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre; Australia: Scamwatch; EU: national consumer or police cybercrime service).\n\n## What to watch for next\n- Follow-up messages that reference the first one (\"we noticed you visited…\", \"your refund is ready\"). Same sender tricks, second attempt.\n- Calls from someone claiming to be the bank or \"fraud department\" asking them to confirm codes or move money. Real banks never ask for a code they sent you.\n- Unexpected password-reset emails or sign-in alerts over the next few weeks: check them in the real app, never through links in the alert.\n\n## Reassurance\nOpening a link on an up-to-date phone or computer very rarely installs anything by itself. The danger is almost always what a page asks you to type or download, and closing it stops that. Lots of careful people click links like this; noticing and asking is exactly the right move.\n",
  entered_password: "# Playbook: entered_password — \"I typed my password somewhere\"\n\nUse when the person typed a password (and maybe a code) into a page that may be fake. Ask which account it was for, and whether they use that same password anywhere else. Those two answers decide the order below.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Go to the real site or app yourself (type the address or use the app, not the link) and change that password. Make it new and unique.\n2. If the same password is used on other accounts, change it there too, starting with email, then banking, then shopping and social media.\n3. Sign out other sessions: most services have \"Sign out of all other devices\" next to the password setting.\n4. Turn on two-step verification (an authenticator app or passkey is best; text codes are better than nothing).\n\n## Then\n- Email account first: whoever controls your email can reset everything else. Check for forwarding rules, filters, and recovery addresses you did not add, and remove them.\n  - Google: Security Checkup at https://myaccount.google.com/security-checkup, and Gmail Settings → Forwarding and filters.\n  - Apple: review devices and trusted phone numbers at https://account.apple.com.\n  - Microsoft: recent activity at https://account.live.com/Activity, then Outlook Settings → Mail → Forwarding and Rules.\n- Check recent sign-ins and connected apps on the affected account and remove anything unfamiliar.\n- If it was a bank or card login: call the number on the back of your card and tell them. Ask them to watch for unusual activity and whether a new card or account number makes sense.\n- Consider a password manager so every account gets its own password; it also refuses to fill passwords on fake sites.\n- If they also entered a one-time code, follow shared_code as well.\n\n## Report\n- Phishing email: \"Report phishing\" in Gmail or Outlook; Apple-themed scams to reportphishing@apple.com.\n- Phishing text: forward it to 7726.\n- The fake page: https://safebrowsing.google.com/safebrowsing/report_phish/.\n- If money or identity is involved: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov (US), or IC3 at https://www.ic3.gov for internet crime. Outside the US, their country's consumer protection agency.\n\n## What to watch for next\n- Password-reset emails they did not ask for, and sign-in alerts from new places. Check them inside the real app.\n- Messages to their contacts from their account asking for money or gift cards: warn friends and family if the account was misused.\n- A \"security team\" or \"refund department\" calling to help: that is the next step of the same scam.\n\n## Reassurance\nA typed password is recoverable in minutes: once it is changed and other sessions are signed out, the old one is useless to whoever has it. Acting within the hour, as they are now, is what matters most.\n",
  sent_gift_cards: "# Playbook: sent_gift_cards — \"I bought gift cards for someone\"\n\nUse when the person bought gift cards and shared the card numbers or photos of them, often because someone claimed to be a boss, relative, government agency, tech support, or a romantic interest. Ask which brand of card and whether the numbers have already been shared. For money cases, suggest involving a trusted person.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Stop: do not buy any more cards and do not send any more numbers, whatever the person on the phone or in the chat says.\n2. Keep the cards and every receipt. Take photos of the back of each card and the receipt.\n3. Contact the card issuer immediately using the support page on its official website or the number printed on the back of the card (not a number the scammer gave). Tell them the card was used in a scam and ask them to freeze any remaining balance.\n4. Stop talking to the scammer. Block the number, email, or account.\n\n## Then\n- Issuer support (use the official site, then its fraud or gift card scam page):\n  - Apple gift cards: support.apple.com (search \"gift card scams\").\n  - Google Play: support.google.com/googleplay.\n  - Amazon: amazon.com customer service, gift card section.\n  - Target and Walmart: the customer service pages on target.com and walmart.com.\n  - Steam: help.steampowered.com.\n- If they paid with a credit or debit card, call the number on the back of that card and explain it was a scam purchase; ask what can be done.\n- Go back to the store where the cards were bought if it is nearby; some stores can contact the issuer faster.\n- Write down what happened while it is fresh: dates, names used, numbers and accounts the scammer used, amounts.\n\n## Report\n- FTC: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov, or call 1-877-382-4357 (US).\n- IC3 (FBI internet crime): https://www.ic3.gov, especially if the loss is large.\n- The scam text that started it: forward it to 7726.\n- Outside the US: their country's consumer protection agency (UK: Action Fraud; Canada: Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre; Australia: Scamwatch).\n\n## What to watch for next\n- \"Recovery\" offers: anyone who contacts them promising to get the money back for a fee is running a second scam.\n- The same scammer returning with a new story (\"there's a problem with the cards, buy more to fix it\").\n- Calls from someone claiming to be the FTC, police, or the card company asking for more money or codes. Real agencies do not ask for gift cards.\n\n## Reassurance\nGift card scams are designed to rush smart, kind people, and they fool a great many every day. Reporting quickly gives the issuer the best chance to freeze what is left, and the report helps stop the same scammer from reaching others.\n",
  shared_code: "# Playbook: shared_code — \"I shared a code\"\n\nUse when the person read out or forwarded a one-time code (text, email, or app prompt) to someone. The code usually lets the other person sign in, reset a password, add a device, or approve a payment. Ask which service the code came from (the text usually names it) and how long ago it was shared.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Open the real app or website for that service and sign in. If you cannot, use its \"forgot password\" or account recovery straight away.\n2. Change the password and choose \"sign out of all other devices\" if offered.\n3. If the code was from a bank, card, or payment app (Zelle, PayPal, Cash App, Venmo), call the number on the back of your card or use the in-app support and tell them a code was shared.\n4. If the code was for WhatsApp, Signal, or another messaging app, re-register the app on your phone now; that kicks the other person off.\n\n## Then\n- Check the account for changes: new devices, new email or phone number on the account, new recovery options, new payees, and remove anything you did not add.\n  - Google: https://myaccount.google.com/security-checkup.\n  - Apple: devices and trusted numbers at https://account.apple.com.\n  - Microsoft: https://account.live.com/Activity.\n- Turn on stronger two-step verification (authenticator app or passkey) and set a PIN or registration lock in messaging apps.\n- If the code came from the phone carrier, call the carrier from another phone or visit a store and ask whether a SIM swap or number port was requested; ask for a port-out PIN.\n- Warn close contacts if a messaging or social account was taken over, so they ignore requests for money.\n\n## Report\n- The message that asked for the code: forward texts to 7726; report email as phishing in the mail app.\n- Losses or account takeover: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov (US) and https://www.ic3.gov.\n- Outside the US: their country's consumer protection agency.\n\n## What to watch for next\n- More codes arriving that they did not request: someone is still trying. Never share them; codes are only ever typed into the real app by you.\n- People claiming to be the service (\"we sent you a code by mistake, please read it back\"). No real company asks for a code it sent.\n- Friends reporting odd messages from the account.\n\n## Reassurance\nA shared code is usually only good for a few minutes and for one action. Signing in, changing the password, and signing out other devices undoes most of what it allowed. Checking now is the right call.\n",
  paid_scammer: "# Playbook: paid_scammer — \"I sent money\"\n\nUse when the person sent money to someone who may be a scammer: bank wire, Zelle, Venmo, Cash App, PayPal, cryptocurrency (including a crypto ATM), or cash (mailed or handed to a courier). Ask how they paid and how long ago; speed matters most for wires and card payments. For money cases, suggest involving a trusted person.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Stop sending. Do not send more, even if told it will \"unlock\" or \"refund\" the first payment.\n2. Call your bank or card issuer using the number on the back of your card (or inside the official app). Say \"I was scammed and need to report fraud\" and ask them to stop or recall the payment.\n3. For payment apps (Zelle, Venmo, Cash App, PayPal), report the payment inside the app and also call your bank, since Zelle runs through it.\n4. Stop contact with the scammer and save everything: messages, phone numbers, account details, receipts, transaction ids.\n\n## Then\n- Wire transfers: ask the bank to send a recall request to the receiving bank today. The sooner, the better the chance.\n- Card payments: ask about a chargeback or dispute.\n- Crypto: contact the exchange or crypto ATM operator through its official website with the transaction id and wallet address. Recovery is hard, so report quickly.\n- Cash sent by mail or courier: if mailed, contact USPS at 1-800-275-8777 (or the shipping company's official support) with the tracking number; they may be able to intercept it.\n- If they shared bank log-in details, change them now and follow entered_password. If the scammer had remote access, follow device_compromised.\n- Consider a free credit freeze with the three US credit bureaus if they shared a Social Security number or ID documents.\n\n## Report\n- FTC: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov, or 1-877-382-4357 (US).\n- IC3 (FBI internet crime): https://www.ic3.gov. Include transaction ids and wallet addresses.\n- Local police for a report number, if the bank asks for one.\n- Outside the US: their country's consumer protection agency (UK: Action Fraud; Canada: Canadian Anti-Fraud Centre; Australia: Scamwatch; EU: national police cybercrime service).\n\n## What to watch for next\n- \"Recovery\" scams: someone claiming to be a lawyer, government agency, or investigator who can recover the money for a fee. Real agencies never charge to recover funds.\n- A \"refund department\" reaching out to say they sent too much back: this is the classic next step.\n- Unexpected accounts or loans in their name if personal details were shared.\n\n## Reassurance\nScammers are professionals who use urgency and fear on purpose; this happens to careful, intelligent people every day. Calling the bank quickly and reporting gives the best chance of stopping the payment and protects others too.\n",
  device_compromised: "# Playbook: device_compromised — \"Someone had remote access to my device\"\n\nUse when a \"tech support\", bank, or refund caller got the person to install a remote-access app (AnyDesk, TeamViewer, UltraViewer, Quick Assist, or similar) or to type a code that let them control the computer or phone. Ask whether they logged into any bank or email account while the caller was connected, and whether money moved.\n\n## First, right now\n1. Disconnect: end the call, then turn off Wi-Fi or unplug the network cable so the remote session stops.\n2. Uninstall the remote-access app (on Windows: Settings → Apps; on a Mac: drag it from Applications to the Bin; on a phone: delete the app).\n3. From a different, trusted device, change the passwords for email and banking, and sign out other sessions.\n4. Call your bank using the number on the back of your card if they logged into banking during the call or shared card details.\n\n## Then\n- Run a full scan: Microsoft Defender on Windows (Windows Security → Virus & threat protection → Scan options → Full scan). On a Mac, keep macOS updated and remove unknown apps and login items (System Settings → General → Login Items).\n- Check for new user accounts, browser extensions, and apps installed during the call; remove them.\n- If you are unsure the device is clean, ask a trusted technician or back up your files and reset the device.\n- Check email for forwarding rules and recovery options you did not add:\n  - Google: https://myaccount.google.com/security-checkup.\n  - Apple: https://account.apple.com.\n  - Microsoft: https://account.live.com/Activity.\n- Turn on two-step verification on email and banking.\n\n## Report\n- FTC: https://reportfraud.ftc.gov (US), or 1-877-382-4357.\n- IC3: https://www.ic3.gov, especially if money moved.\n- If the caller claimed to be Microsoft or Apple, those companies accept scam reports through their official support websites.\n- Outside the US: their country's consumer protection agency.\n\n## What to watch for next\n- The same people calling back (\"we saw a problem\", \"we refunded too much\"). Hang up; real companies do not make unsolicited tech-support calls.\n- Pop-ups with a phone number to call. Close the browser; never call numbers from pop-ups.\n- Unexpected transfers or new payees in online banking over the coming weeks.\n\n## Reassurance\nCutting the connection and removing the app stops their access. Changing passwords from a clean device closes the doors they may have seen. Many people are caught by these calls because they sound so official; asking for help now is exactly right.\n",
} as const;
